"Let $G=(V,E)$ be a possibly infinite graph and let $T\\subseteq V$. A system $\\mathcal{P}$ of vertex-disjoint $T$-paths and a set $X\\subseteq V$ are required to satisfy: for every component $Y$ of $G-X$, the paths contained in $Y$ cover all but at most one vertex of $T\\cap Y$, and $X\\subseteq V(\\mathcal{P})$ with $|V(P)\\cap X|\\leq 1$ for every $P\\in\\mathcal{P}$.\n\n**Gallai's conjecture.** There exists such a system $\\mathcal{P}$ and set $X$.\n\nThis is proposed as an infinite generalization of Gallai's theorem; the supplied text does not state that it has been proved."